Examples for the ILVES bond/angle constraint solver (fix ilves).

These inputs mirror the standard examples/peptide setup but use
fix ilves instead of fix shake to apply rigid bond / virtual-angle
constraints.  The same selectors (b/a/t/m) are accepted, so the
fix shake usage:

    fix cstr all shake 1.0e-4 25 0 b 4 6 8 10 12 14 18 a 31

becomes:

    fix cstr all ilves 1.0e-4 25 0 b 4 6 8 10 12 14 18 a 31

Unlike fix shake, fix ilves handles arbitrarily large connected
constraint clusters (e.g. all C-C backbone bonds of a long polymer
chain in a single cluster), not just small clusters of up to 4 atoms.

fix ilves works correctly across MPI ranks for both newton on and
newton off settings -- the constraint topology is gathered globally
at init, and cross-rank clusters are fully replicated on every
participating rank so that all ranks compute the same Lagrange
multipliers and each applies the constraint force to its local
atoms only.

Two solver variants are available via the *variant* keyword:
  * variant = ilves  (default): symmetric Jacobian using r_k.r_l in
    off-diagonals; the matrix is true-symmetric (could in principle
    be solved with Cholesky for a constant-factor speedup)
  * variant = ilvesf : the structurally-symmetric exact Newton
    Jacobian using s_k.r_l in off-diagonals

Both variants converge to the exact solution of the constraint
equations; ilves uses a quasi-Newton step that is slightly cheaper
to assemble per iteration.

Example input files:
  in.peptide-ilves    -- peptide-in-water test with ILVES solver, mirrors examples/peptide/in.peptide
  in.peptide-ilvesf   -- peptide-in-water test with ILVES-F solver, mirrors examples/peptide/in.peptide
  in.peptide-mix      -- peptide-in-water test with ILVES and SHAKE for water, mirrors examples/peptide/in.peptide
  in.peptide-shake    -- peptide-in-water test with SHAKE for comparison with same settings
  in.peptide-rattle   -- peptide-in-water test with RATTLE for comparison with same settings
  in.peptide-allbonds -- peptide-in-water test with ILVES solver, *all* bonds are constrained
